      *Abstract* — A settlement architecture for peer-to-peer (P2P) energy
      trading must sustain continuous device telemetry, guarantee that traded
      energy value is conserved, and remain auditable as the community grows.
      We evaluate GridTokenX — six cooperating Anchor programs on a
      permissioned Solana runtime — by replaying one simulated week of
      *physically-modelled* community electricity through its complete
      lifecycle (telemetry ingest, GRID token mint, continuous-double-auction
      order entry, dual-signature off-chain-match settlement, burn-on-delivery,
      and renewable-energy-certificate issuance) at four community sizes:
      80, 160, 380, and 760 smart meters, each with a 15% rooftop-solar prosumer
      share. Energy data is generated by device-level models — clear-sky
      photovoltaic generation @pvlib, voltage-dependent ZIP loads, and full
      alternating-current power flow @pandapower solved on an 80-bus
      distribution network every fifteen minutes. Every headline figure is
      re-derived from live chain state by an independent on-chain auditor. We
      find that telemetry throughput scales *super-linearly* with fleet size
      (230 to 1,016 readings per second) because hot-path state resides in
      disjoint per-entity program-derived accounts (PDAs); that energy
      conservation — minted equals settled equals burned — closes to the
      atomic watt-hour on uncontended runs; and that settlement is the system
      bottleneck, gated by a single global write-lock at approximately
      0.6 transactions per second. A controlled re-run isolates this
      bottleneck as contention-driven rather than a protocol defect, and
      demonstrates that the on-chain auditor reliably detects the resulting
      state divergence. Total wall-clock compression of a simulated week
      ranges from 993× to 510×.
    ]
  ]
  #v(0.4em)
]

= 1. Introduction

Thailand's draft Power Development Plan (PDP 2026–2050) @moe_factsheet_2026
initiates Direct Power Purchase Agreement frameworks that will, for the first
time, allow distributed rooftop-solar prosumers to contract with one another.
Executing such agreements at P2P scale imposes requirements a general-purpose
blockchain does not meet by default: high-frequency ingest from many devices,
provable dual-authorised and non-replayable settlement, and physical backing
so that a kilowatt-hour token traces to an attested measurement rather than
being minted at will — a shortcoming of early microgrid pilots @ref3.
GridTokenX @ref1 addresses these by routing every hot-path write to a
per-entity PDA, so transactions with disjoint write sets execute in parallel.

A prior study established feasibility on a single 80-meter community. This
report answers the next question — *how the layer behaves as the community
grows* — and is organised around four questions: what is tested (§2, the test
case), how it works (§3), how each result is verified (§4), and what remains
(§5, future work). The contributions are: (i) a physics-grounded, deterministic
dataset generator that drives the on-chain programs from modelled solar, load,
and grid power flow; (ii) an automated per-fleet harness that provisions a fresh
validator, runs the lifecycle, and audits the outcome against chain state; and
(iii) an empirical characterisation of the scaling limits, isolating settlement
contention from a distinct trading-layer effect.

= 2. The Test Case

The test case is a run matrix that varies community size while holding the
generation model, seed, prosumer share, market rules, and horizon fixed, so that
any change in outcome is attributable to scale alone (@tbl-matrix). Three primary
scenarios span a 4.75× range — 160, 380, and 760 meters — each at a 15%
rooftop-solar prosumer share under a 10 kWh per-day sell cap, trading across 16
zone shards over a 7-day horizon of 96 fifteen-minute ticks per day. A fourth
scenario re-runs the 380-meter case on a *quiescent* host to isolate
host-contention effects (§4.3), and a reduced 20-meter, 2-day scenario serves as
a pipeline smoke test that validates the export-to-audit chain before the
primary matrix. Each scenario provisions a fresh validator and is audited
independently, so no run's state can influence another.

