*3.3 Pricing and settlement.* Price formation is bounded before any matching:
every limit price must satisfy $p_min <= p <= p_max$ at admission, on-chain
matches cross at the seller's limit $p^* = p_s$, and an off-chain-matched trade
re-enforces the bounds *both counterparties signed*, $p_s <= p^* <= p_b$, so no
party settles worse than the limit it authorised. All monetary arithmetic is
exact-integer with floored division on two fixed-point scales: energy $q$ in
atomic units (kilowatt-hours scaled by $10^9$) and price $p^*$ in $10^6$ minor
units of the baht-pegged settlement currency; the gross value divides their
$10^15$-scaled product by the energy scale $10^9$ — omitting this divisor would
overpay the seller $10^9$-fold. A match of quantity $q$ at price $p^*$ yields the
gross value $V$, a market fee $F_m$ ($phi_m$ basis points; $phi_m = 25$ by
default), a *flat-rate* wheeling charge $C_w$ ($w_r$ per kilowatt-hour), and a
line-loss charge $C_ell$ ($ell$ basis points):

$ V &= floor(frac(q p^*, 10^9)), &quad F_m &= floor(frac(V phi_m, 10^4)), \
  C_w &= floor(frac(q w_r, 10^9)), &quad C_ell &= floor(frac(V ell, 10^4)) $ <eq-settle>

Wheeling is per kilowatt-hour, not a fraction of value, since delivery cost does
not scale with the agreed price; $w_r$ and $ell$ come from an on-chain tariff
schedule movable only by the network authorities. The seller takes the residual
under saturating subtraction, and the four transfers exhaust the buyer's outflow
exactly:

$ N_"seller" = (V - F_m - C_w - C_ell)^+, quad
  V = N_"seller" + F_m + C_w + C_ell $ <eq-net>

where $(dot)^+ = max(dot, 0)$. The conservation and compression identities the
system maintains are

$ E_"mint" = E_"settle" = E_"burn", quad E_"mint" + E_"REC" <= E_"gen", quad
  C = (D dot 86400) / T_"wall", $ <eq-conserve>

for a simulated horizon of $D$ days absorbed in $T_"wall"$ seconds. Replay safety
follows from a per-order nullifier recording cumulative fill, so a signed order
may fill partially but never over-fill or replay; certificate retirement burns
REC base units ($10^3$ per kilowatt-hour), preventing double-counting.

= 4. Verifying the Test Case

Verification rests on independence: no reported figure originates from the
program that produced it. After each run a separate auditor re-derives every
quantity — PDA counts, per-meter reading sums, registry settled and claimed
generation, token supplies, and currency conservation — from remote-procedure-call
reads of chain state, accepting the run only on an exact match (@fig-results
summarises the outcomes). All runs used a single-node validator (Solana 3.1.10)
on an Apple M2 host.

