*4.1 Telemetry scales super-linearly above a small-fleet floor.* @tbl-tel
reports confirmed oracle throughput. Across the three larger fleets each
doubling more than doubles throughput: readings land in disjoint per-meter PDAs,
so larger fleets expose more independent transaction chains that amortise the
single gateway fee-payer. The smallest 80-meter fleet sits *below* this trend at
176.8 readings per second — under 160 meters, too few concurrent meter chains
exist to saturate the fee-payer, so throughput is chain-starved rather than
oracle-bound (the 80-meter run also shared its host, unlike the idle larger-fleet
runs). Delivery loss is zero at every scale; all rejections are the anomaly gate
operating correctly (fee-paid, on-ledger), and the 380-meter split reproduced
bit-identically across runs. The per-reading compute cost stays scale-invariant
at approximately 13–15 thousand compute units (CU) — 15.0k at 80 meters, squarely
in range.

