*4.3 Settlement is the bottleneck, and it is contention-driven.* The 380-meter
case first ran under host contention and broke conservation: 57 settlement
transactions reached the confirm-timeout, stranding 570 kWh of minted GRID as
un-burned supply ($E_"mint" = 3178.3 != 2608.3 = E_"burn"$). The auditor
flagged the divergence precisely — on-chain GRID supply after burns of
570,323 Wh against 323 Wh expected — from chain reads alone. Re-run on an
idle host, *all 57 failures vanished*: the layer settled 345 of 345 matches
and the registry's settled generation equalled GRID minted to the watt-hour.
@tbl-380 contrasts the two runs. Settlement serialises on a global
$"treasury\_state"$ and fee-collector write-lock (approximately 0.6
transactions per second) irrespective of shard; this lock has no slack under
contention, which is the mechanism behind the timeouts.

