*4.4 A distinct trading-layer residual.* Removing contention fixed settlement
entirely but exposed a second, smaller and independent limit. Even idle, the
trading layer dropped 5 of 2,609 order submissions (0.2%) — a burst-contention
artifact of the $"zone\_market"$ write-lock that serialises shard submits —
leaving 30 kWh (0.9% of traded energy) of minted GRID unmatched and therefore
unburned. The strict token-supply audit correctly flags this residual
(GRID supply 30,323 Wh against 323 Wh expected). It is a trading-layer, not
settlement-layer, limitation; the 760-meter case shows the same effect at
16 of 5,219 orders (0.3%).

*4.5 Wall-clock and compression.* A simulated week (604,800 s) is absorbed in
minutes: 971× at 80 m (623 s), 993× at 160 m (609 s), 789× at 380 m idle
(767 s), and 510× at 760 m (1,186 s). The 80-meter figure trails the 160-meter
one despite the smaller fleet because its chain-starved telemetry (§4.1) lengthens
the replay phase, not because of settlement. Above that floor, compression falls
as the fixed settlement ceiling comes to dominate lifecycle time, yet the larger
760-meter fleet still completes quickly on an idle host — confirming that the
contended 380-meter figure reflected host load, not fleet size.

*4.6 Prosumer revenue and network charges.* These scaling runs *fix* the
settlement price rather than discover it: every match executes at 4.00
settlement-currency units per kilowatt-hour, within the signed bounds
$[3.90, 4.50]$ the on-chain settlement enforces. Dynamic price discovery through
the continuous double auction is exercised only for order-entry throughput
(§4.1) and is out of scope here. Settled *value* therefore scales linearly with
settled *energy* (@tbl-rev). The buyer's gross outflow is split by @eq-net into
the prosumer's net proceeds and network charges. The test tariff sets a market
fee of 25 basis points, a line-loss rate of 5 basis points, and a flat wheeling
rate of 0.10 per kilowatt-hour; but because charges are floored to integer minor
units and the per-match gross value is small (about 37 units), the fee and loss
round *down to zero* at this granularity, leaving only the per-kilowatt-hour
wheeling charge — larger, and surviving the floor — collected. Prosumers
consequently capture a near-constant $97.6$–$97.7%$ of gross value, with a
$approx 2.3%$ wheeling residual, and per-prosumer revenue is stable ($227$, $215$,
$217$, and $218$ units over the week at 80, 160, 380, and 760 meters; the smallest
fleet's dozen prosumers each settle marginally more energy, hence a slightly
higher figure) — scaling the community neither dilutes nor concentrates individual
returns. Renewable-energy certificates (1.3, 2.6, 6.8, and 14.2 MWh certified) are
a *separate, non-monetised* value stream:
the cap-withheld surplus retained as an environmental claim rather than sold, and
excluded from the revenue figures.

