*4.8 Reproducibility.* All runs use commit `1895458` with Solana 3.1.10 and a
fixed generation seed (42); datasets, per-case artifacts, and audit logs are
retained under `test-results/`. Historical timestamps and the fixed seed make
each dataset reproducible, and the telemetry outcome (confirmed versus
anomaly-rejected reading counts) reproduces bit-identically across runs. To
separate signal from host noise, each scenario is executed $N = 3$ times and
throughput is reported as the median with observed range; compute-unit costs are
medians, scale-invariant per operation and robust to load. The full matrix is one
command — `CASES="160:24 380:57 760:114" DAYS=7 bash scripts/run-scale-sweep.sh`
— which per case exports the physics dataset, provisions a fresh validator,
deploys the five programs, runs the lifecycle, and audits against chain state.
The §4.7 price-model comparison is likewise one command
(`scripts/run-price-models-onchain.sh`), with its seven-check verifier
(`scripts/verify-price-models-onchain.ts`) re-run against the *persisted*
ledger after the run, so the checks read state the runner can no longer touch.

*4.9 Deriving each reported quantity.* Every headline number is a closed-form
function of stated inputs, so a reader can reproduce it by hand rather than trust
the harness. For $M$ meters, $D$ days at interval $Delta$ seconds, telemetry rate
$overline(r)$ over wall time $T_"tel"$, settled energy $q$ (kWh) at fixed price
$p^*$ with wheeling rate $w_r$, the mapping is

$ R &= M dot D dot 86400 / Delta, &quad overline(r) &= R_"ok" / T_"tel", \
  V &= sum_i floor(frac(q_i p^*, 10^9)) approx p^* q, &quad C_w &approx w_r q, \
  N_"seller" &= V - F_m - C_w - C_ell, &quad eta &= N_"seller" / V, \
  "REC"_"base" &= 10^3 dot E_"REC"^"kWh", &quad C &= D dot 86400 / T_"wall" $ <eq-verify>

each mapping to exactly one auditor check. *Worked example, 380 m (idle).*
Readings 380 × 7 × 96 = 255,360, matching @tbl-matrix; confirmed
255,360 − 1,353 = 254,007 after the deterministic anomaly count; rate
254,007 ÷ 351.4 = 722.9 per second (@tbl-tel). Revenue: gross 12,686 (from price
$p^* = 4.00$ over $q = 3178.3$ kWh — the per-match floor trims $p^* q = 12713$
by 27); wheeling 298 (from $w_r = 0.10$, floored from 317.8); with fee and loss
floored to zero, seller net 12,686 − 298 = 12,388 and capture 97.7% (@tbl-rev).
Certificate base units $10^3 times 6804.2$ = 6,804,214,000, matching the audited
REC supply. Compression (7 × 86,400) ÷ 766.5 = 789× (§4.5). Each equals its
independently audited on-chain value.

*4.10 Threats to validity.* Three limits bound the claims. (i) *Deployment* —
results are single-host and single-validator; a multi-node permissioned cluster
would shift the fee-payer and lock ceilings, so the throughput figures are a
floor for that topology, not a projection to production. (ii) *Host sensitivity*
— the 380-meter contention episode shows throughput is sensitive to host load;
figures are therefore gathered on a quiescent machine and reported with
repetition (§4.8). (iii) *Feeder sizing* — the physical layer models each meter's
generation and consumption realistically (photovoltaic and ZIP), but the shared
80-bus test feeder is deliberately oversubscribed at the upper fleet sizes (from
about two meters per bus at 160 m to roughly ten at 760 m). Consequently
bus-level quantities are stressed — at 380 m the solved network shows minimum
voltage near 0.72 pu and transformer loading of 192–209% — and are reported
as-is, not as validated distribution operation. This does not affect the
per-meter energy time-series that drives the on-chain lifecycle, but it means the
grid-physics outputs characterise an intentionally undersized feeder.

= 5. Future Work

The measured limits map directly to the next iteration. *Widen the settlement
ceiling.* Settlement's ~0.6-transactions-per-second global write-lock on
$"treasury\_state"$ and the fee collectors is the dominant bottleneck (§4.3);
fee-payer pooling and sharding the collector accounts would let disjoint matches
settle in parallel, as telemetry already does. *Harden order entry.* The sub-1%
$"zone\_market"$ order-drop under burst load (§4.4) calls for submit-retry with
back-pressure so no signed order is silently lost. *Enforce the network-charge
cap.* The settlement model bounds charges by construction, but a 20% cap on the
combined network charge is defined yet not wired into the off-chain-match path;
enabling it would make the bound hold on chain, not only by convention.
*Dynamic price discovery at fleet scale.* The scaling runs fix the execution
price (§4.6); §4.7 evaluates discovered prices on an aggregated tranche book.
Exercising the continuous double auction for settlement per order, at fleet
scale — rather than on the reduced book a single `clear_auction` transaction
admits — is the natural next study, as is sharding the auction book itself.
*Multi-node and compliant feeder.* Repeating the matrix on a multi-node
permissioned cluster would lift the single-fee-payer and single-validator
ceilings, and right-sizing the distribution feeder would bring the grid-physics
outputs within IEEE operating bounds (§4.10). Finally, aggregating sub-unit fees
would recover the fee and loss revenue that per-match flooring currently discards.

= 6. Conclusion

Across a 4.75× range in community size, the GridTokenX settlement layer sustains
super-linearly scaling telemetry, conserves traded energy value to the atomic
watt-hour, and remains fully auditable from chain state. Its dominant scaling
limit is a contention-sensitive global settlement lock, beneath which a
sub-1% trading-layer order-drop resides. Both are localised and addressable, and
neither compromises the conservation guarantee on a quiescent host. The
physics-grounded harness and per-fleet auditor introduced here provide a
repeatable basis for evaluating the next architectural iteration.

